{
  "id": "m3l3",
  "title": {
    "es": "Tuplas y conjuntos",
    "en": "Tuples and sets"
  },
  "theory": {
    "es": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Tuplas: colecciones inmutables"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Una **tupla** es como una lista pero **no se puede modificar** una vez creada. Se escribe entre paréntesis. Se usa para datos que van juntos y no deberían cambiar, como unas coordenadas."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "punto = (3, 4)",
          "print(punto[0])",
          "print(punto[1])"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "3",
          "4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Una función puede devolver varios valores a la vez como una tupla, y se pueden 'desempaquetar' en varias variables."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "def minmax(lista):",
          "    return min(lista), max(lista)",
          "",
          "menor, mayor = minmax([4, 1, 7])",
          "print(menor, mayor)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "1 7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Conjuntos: elementos únicos"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Un **conjunto** (`set`) es una colección **sin duplicados y sin orden**, entre llaves. Convertir una lista a `set` elimina los repetidos al instante."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "s = {1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3}",
          "print(s)",
          "print(len(s))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "{1, 2, 3}",
          "3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Operaciones de conjuntos"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Los conjuntos permiten operaciones matemáticas: `&` (intersección, lo común), `|` (unión, todo) y `-` (diferencia)."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "a = {1, 2, 3}",
          "b = {2, 3, 4}",
          "print(a & b)",
          "print(a | b)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "{2, 3}",
          "{1, 2, 3, 4}"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "value": "Comprobar si algo está en un `set` es muy rápido (mucho más que en una lista grande). Úsalos cuando solo te importe la pertenencia y la unicidad."
      }
    ],
    "en": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Tuples: immutable collections"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "A **tuple** is like a list but **cannot be modified** once created. It is written with parentheses. Use it for data that belongs together and shouldn't change, like coordinates."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "point = (3, 4)",
          "print(point[0])",
          "print(point[1])"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "3",
          "4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "A function can return several values at once as a tuple, and they can be 'unpacked' into several variables."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "def minmax(items):",
          "    return min(items), max(items)",
          "",
          "low, high = minmax([4, 1, 7])",
          "print(low, high)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "1 7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Sets: unique elements"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "A **set** is a collection **without duplicates and without order**, between braces. Converting a list to a `set` removes repeats instantly."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "s = {1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3}",
          "print(s)",
          "print(len(s))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "{1, 2, 3}",
          "3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Set operations"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Sets allow mathematical operations: `&` (intersection, the common ones), `|` (union, everything) and `-` (difference)."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "a = {1, 2, 3}",
          "b = {2, 3, 4}",
          "print(a & b)",
          "print(a | b)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "{2, 3}",
          "{1, 2, 3, 4}"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "value": "Checking membership in a `set` is very fast (much faster than in a large list). Use them when you only care about membership and uniqueness."
      }
    ]
  },
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "m3l3e1",
      "prompt": {
        "es": "Define `unicos(lista)` que devuelva una lista **ordenada** con los elementos sin repetir. Ejemplo: `[3, 1, 3, 2, 1]` -> `[1, 2, 3]`.",
        "en": "Define `unicos(lista)` returning a **sorted** list with the elements without repeats. Example: `[3, 1, 3, 2, 1]` -> `[1, 2, 3]`."
      },
      "starter_code": [
        "def unicos(lista):",
        "    pass"
      ],
      "solution": [
        "def unicos(lista):",
        "    return sorted(set(lista))"
      ],
      "hints": {
        "es": [
          "set(lista) elimina duplicados.",
          "sorted(...) devuelve una lista ordenada."
        ],
        "en": [
          "set(lista) removes duplicates.",
          "sorted(...) returns a sorted list."
        ]
      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "type": "function",
          "name": "unicos",
          "function": "unicos",
          "cases": [
            {
              "args": [
                [
                  3,
                  1,
                  3,
                  2,
                  1
                ]
              ],
              "expected": [
                1,
                2,
                3
              ]
            },
            {
              "args": [
                []
              ],
              "expected": []
            },
            {
              "args": [
                [
                  5,
                  5,
                  5
                ]
              ],
              "expected": [
                5
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "m3l3e2",
      "prompt": {
        "es": "Define `minmax(numeros)` que devuelva una **tupla** `(menor, mayor)` con el mínimo y el máximo de la lista (no vacía).",
        "en": "Define `minmax(numeros)` returning a **tuple** `(min, max)` with the smallest and largest of the (non-empty) list."
      },
      "starter_code": [
        "def minmax(numeros):",
        "    pass"
      ],
      "solution": [
        "def minmax(numeros):",
        "    return (min(numeros), max(numeros))"
      ],
      "hints": {
        "es": [
          "Puedes usar min() y max().",
          "Devuélvelos como tupla: return (min(...), max(...))"
        ],
        "en": [
          "You can use min() and max().",
          "Return them as a tuple: return (min(...), max(...))"
        ]
      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "name": "minmax devuelve tupla",
          "harness": "assert minmax([4, 1, 7]) == (1, 7), 'minmax([4,1,7]) debe ser (1, 7)'\nassert minmax([5]) == (5, 5)\nassert minmax([-3, -1, -9]) == (-9, -1)\nassert isinstance(minmax([1, 2]), tuple), 'debe devolver una tupla'"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}